const articles={"1": "<p>SAP Basis remains the operating layer of the enterprise: the people who install, secure, transport, monitor, and recover the systems that finance, supply chain, and manufacturing depend on. The role has not become smaller in the S/4HANA era. It has become broader. Hiring managers now expect a consultant who can explain a client strategy, a transport path, a HANA savepoint, and a cloud landing zone in the same conversation.</p>\n<p>This roadmap is the sequence Zyntegra uses with learners who want to be credible in production, not merely familiar with transaction codes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-role-actually-is\">What the role actually is</h2>\n<p>A Basis professional owns the technical integrity of the SAP landscape. That includes system architecture, clients, users and authorizations, the transport management system, background processing, performance, backup and recovery, high availability, and the operating system and database underneath the application. In 2026 it also includes cloud placement (Azure, AWS, or Google Cloud), S/4HANA conversion readiness, and a working knowledge of SAP Business Technology Platform integration patterns.</p>\n<p>If you cannot describe how a change moves from development to production, and what you would do when that production system stops responding at 02:00, you are not yet interview-ready — regardless of how many courses you have bookmarked.</p>\n<h2 id=\"phase-1-foundations-weeks-1-4\">Phase 1 — Foundations (weeks 1–4)</h2>\n<p>Start with the landscape, not with tools.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Learn the three-system model: development, quality, and production, and why configuration and workbench objects travel in different ways.</li>\n<li>Become fluent in SAP GUI navigation, client concepts, and the difference between a client-dependent table and a cross-client object.</li>\n<li>Practice user administration until creating a role, tracing a missing authorization with SU53, and locking a dialog user are routine.</li>\n<li>Introduce the Transport Management System at a conceptual level: domain controller, transport routes, and the cost of an import into the wrong system.</li>\n<li>Build a daily monitoring habit: work processes, short dumps, system log, update failures, and locks.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"practice\"><strong>Practice standard:</strong> you can draw your landscape on a whiteboard and walk a new user from logon to a successful transport release without notes.</div>\n<h2 id=\"phase-2-operations-weeks-5-9\">Phase 2 — Operations (weeks 5–9)</h2>\n<p>This is where most junior administrators stall, because the work looks administrative and is actually risk management.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Installation and post-installation: profiles, licenses, system parameters, and the checks you run before anyone is allowed to log on.</li>\n<li>Kernel and support package strategy: what you read in the note, what you snapshot, and how you back out.</li>\n<li>Client copy and system refresh: when a local client copy is enough, and when a full system refresh with post-processing (BDLS, RFC destinations, batch jobs, printers) is required.</li>\n<li>Background jobs and spool: house-keeping jobs, failed chains, and why a full spool filesystem takes a system down as surely as a database outage.</li>\n<li>Backup and restore: what is in the backup set (database, kernel, profiles, transport directory) and how you would prove a restore, not merely schedule one.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"practice\"><strong>Practice standard:</strong> given a written incident, you can name the first three transactions or operating-system checks and the evidence you would capture.</div>\n<h2 id=\"phase-3-enterprise-landscapes-weeks-10-14\">Phase 3 — Enterprise landscapes (weeks 10–14)</h2>\n<p>Move from “keeping the system up” to “explaining why it behaves.”</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Performance: dialog response time, expensive SQL, work-process exhaustion, and the difference between an application problem and a Basis problem.</li>\n<li>High availability and disaster recovery: single points of failure, enqueue replication, HANA system replication, and a recovery time objective you can defend.</li>\n<li>SAP HANA administration at the level a Basis consultant is expected to operate: services, backups, alerts, tenant databases, and sizing conversations with the database team.</li>\n<li>Solution Manager or its successor monitoring stack: early-watch style health, not a wall of unowned dashboards.</li>\n<li>Security hardening: password policy, RFC gateway, web dispatcher, patch cadence, and audit evidence.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"practice\"><strong>Practice standard:</strong> you can brief a delivery manager in ten minutes on risk, impact, and the next safe action.</div>\n<h2 id=\"phase-4-cloud-and-transformation-weeks-15-18\">Phase 4 — Cloud and transformation (weeks 15–18)</h2>\n<p>Cloud does not replace Basis. It relocates it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Know how SAP is hosted on Azure, AWS, and Google Cloud: certified instance families, shared storage, backup services, and network paths to identity and integration.</li>\n<li>Treat S/4HANA conversion readiness as a technical program: custom code, add-ons, simplification items, downtime strategy, and the Basis workstream inside it.</li>\n<li>Automation: repeatable refresh, patching, and health checks. If a step is performed by memory every quarter, it belongs in a runbook or a script.</li>\n<li>Integration: BTP subaccounts, destinations, and Cloud Integration (CPI) at the level required to support connectivity, certificates, and failed messages — even if you are not the integration developer.</li>\n<li>War-room conduct: one timeline, one owner, evidence before opinion, and a written restoration of service.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"interview-preparation-certification-and-how-you-present-yourself\">Interview preparation, certification, and how you present yourself</h2>\n<p>Sequence credentials after competence. Product certifications (C_TADM family and the current S/4HANA technology associate tracks) signal study discipline. They do not replace a story about a refresh, an upgrade, or an outage.</p>\n<p>Prepare five project stories in the STAR shape, each with a metric: downtime avoided, refresh cycle reduced, audit finding closed, queue time improved, or a failed import recovered. Put the same stories on a one-page resume and in the LinkedIn “About” section. Use job language: landscape, transport path, HANA, high availability, cloud migration, stakeholder communication.</p>\n<p>Mock interviews should be scenario-based. Reciting the menu path of STMS is not a senior answer. Explaining what you check before you import a transport during month-end close is.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-90-day-plan-if-you-are-starting-now\">A 90-day plan if you are starting now</h2>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Days</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Focus</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Evidence you keep</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>1–30</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Architecture, clients, users, TMS, daily monitoring</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Landscape diagram and a personal operations checklist</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>31–60</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Install concepts, upgrades, refresh, jobs, backup</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>One written runbook for refresh or backup verification</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>61–90</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Performance, HA/DR, HANA overview, one cloud pattern, two mock interviews</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>A scorecard of gaps and a revised resume</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-ignore-until-the-core-is-solid\">What to ignore until the core is solid</h2>\n<p>Do not start with Fiori freestyle development, BTP extension suites, or a second cloud provider. Do not collect certificates in modules you cannot support operationally. Depth in Basis, HANA, and one cloud platform will outperform a shallow tour of the entire SAP catalog.</p>\n\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Days</th><th>Focus</th><th>Evidence you keep</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>1–30</td><td>Architecture, clients, users, TMS, daily monitoring</td><td>Landscape diagram and a personal operations checklist</td></tr>\n<tr><td>31–60</td><td>Install concepts, upgrades, refresh, jobs, backup</td><td>One written runbook for refresh or backup verification</td></tr>\n<tr><td>61–90</td><td>Performance, HA/DR, HANA overview, one cloud pattern, two mock interviews</td><td>A scorecard of gaps and a revised resume</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"closing-view\">Closing view</h2>\n<p>The consultants who progress in 2026 are the ones who can operate a system and explain the decision. Follow the sequence: foundations, operations, enterprise resilience, then cloud and transformation. Use mock interviews to discover what you cannot yet say clearly. That clarity is the actual deliverable of the roadmap.</p>", "2": "<p>Moving an SAP landscape to Azure, AWS, or Google Cloud is a technical migration and a control migration. The application still needs clients, transports, batch windows, interfaces, printers, and a database that can be restored. The platform underneath changes: identity, network, storage, backup, and the contract that defines who is responsible when the virtual machine is healthy and SAP is not.</p>\n<p>Use this checklist with program leads, Basis owners, and cloud engineers in the same room. An item is done only when the evidence exists, not when a slide says it is done.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-decide-the-landing-pattern-before-you-size-anything\">1. Decide the landing pattern before you size anything</h2>\n<p>Confirm, in writing:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Target platform and region, including paired region for disaster recovery.</li>\n<li>Deployment pattern: rehost (lift-and-shift), replatform (certified cloud infrastructure with current operating system and database), or a combined move with an S/4HANA conversion. Do not hide a conversion inside a migration plan.</li>\n<li>Which systems move in the first wave (typically sandbox and development) and which stay until cutover (production and the interfaces it depends on).</li>\n<li>RTO and RPO agreed with the business, not assumed by the project team.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If production RPO is fifteen minutes, a nightly snapshot policy is already a failed design.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-inventory-the-landscape-as-it-actually-runs\">2. Inventory the landscape as it actually runs</h2>\n<p>Capture more than the SID list.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Systems, clients, products, kernel, database version, and operating system.</li>\n<li>Host sizing: CPU, memory, storage throughput, and growth over the last twelve months.</li>\n<li>Interfaces: RFC, IDoc, files, CPI or PI, third-party tax, banks, warehouses, and SSO.</li>\n<li>Batch schedule, month-end peaks, and print or output management.</li>\n<li>Transport domain, CHARM or change process, and who may import.</li>\n<li>Current backup product, last successful restore test, and where the backup catalog lives.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Interfaces that nobody owns are the usual reason a technically successful cutover is a business failure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-security-and-identity\">3. Security and identity</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Network segmentation: SAP application subnet, database subnet, management subnet, and a controlled path for SAP GUI, Fiori, and administrators.</li>\n<li>No direct exposure of the database port to the internet. Administrative access through a bastion or private connectivity.</li>\n<li>Identity: corporate IdP for administrators, SAP user master still governed inside the application, and a named break-glass account with monitoring on its use.</li>\n<li>Key management for storage encryption and backup encryption, with owners.</li>\n<li>Certificate inventory for web dispatcher, STRUST, RFC SNC, and any Cloud Integration endpoints that will change hostname.</li>\n<li>Logging retained long enough for your audit and incident process.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-sizing-and-architecture\">4. Sizing and architecture</h2>\n<p>Use current workload, not the original installation sheet.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Choose certified instance families for SAP HANA or the database you run. Confirm the cloud provider’s SAP note or certified list for the exact version.</li>\n<li>Separate data, log, and backup volumes where the database design requires it. Shared storage for /sapmnt, /usr/sap, and the transport directory must survive a single host failure.</li>\n<li>Plan enqueue and message server placement if you run application servers separately from the database.</li>\n<li>Right-size non-production. Development does not need production-class HANA memory unless a test demands it.</li>\n<li>Include a documented path to scale up before quarter-end, not after the first incident.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-data-protection\">5. Data protection</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Backup of database, kernel, profiles, and the transport directory.</li>\n<li>Backup jobs monitored, with failure alerting to a person, not only to a dashboard.</li>\n<li>One restore test on the new platform before production cutover, including a point-in-time target if that is your RPO.</li>\n<li>Snapshots are not a backup strategy until you have restored from them and documented the timing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-migration-method-and-downtime\">6. Migration method and downtime</h2>\n<p>Select the method to match database size and the downtime window: backup/restore, HANA system replication, or a provider migration service. For each method record:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Expected transfer duration and the last full rehearsal result.</li>\n<li>Freeze points: transports, client opening, batch jobs, interface queues, and user lock.</li>\n<li>The sequence that starts SAP, validates work processes, and opens the system to a pilot group before the full population.</li>\n<li>A rollback decision time. If you do not name the clock time at which you revert, you do not have a rollback plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"7-cutover-readiness-review\">7. Cutover readiness review</h2>\n<p>Hold this review at least once before the production weekend. Each line needs an owner.</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Check</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Owner</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Evidence</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Target hosts built from the agreed image</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Cloud</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Build record</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>SAP parameters and mounts verified</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Basis</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Checklist signed</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Interfaces pointed at the new endpoints</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Integration</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Test IDoc or message</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>SSO and SAP GUI / Fiori entry tested</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Security</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Pilot user logon</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Backup job successful on target</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Basis</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Job log</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Monitoring and alerts routed</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Operations</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Test alert received</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Hypercare roster published</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Program</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Named shifts</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Rollback tested or explicitly accepted</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Program</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Decision record</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-hypercare\">8. Hypercare</h2>\n<p>The first ten business days are part of the migration.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compare dialog response, failed updates, dumps, and batch overruns with the pre-move baseline.</li>\n<li>Watch interface queues twice daily until the first month-end passes.</li>\n<li>Keep the old landscape recoverable until the business accepts the new one in writing.</li>\n<li>Convert every hypercare incident into a runbook change. A migration that does not update operations documentation will be repeated as folklore.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"common-failures\">Common failures</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Treating the cloud migration as a server move and discovering printers, file interfaces, and STRUST certificates on Saturday night.</li>\n<li>Copying production sizing onto every system and then missing storage throughput, which certified vCPU counts do not guarantee.</li>\n<li>Declaring success at database restore, before a user posts a document and an interface partner acknowledges it.</li>\n<li>Leaving the transport directory and /sapmnt on a single disk with no backup because “the database backup is enough.”</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>Owner</th><th>Evidence</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Target hosts built from the agreed image</td><td>Cloud</td><td>Build record</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SAP parameters and mounts verified</td><td>Basis</td><td>Checklist signed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interfaces pointed at the new endpoints</td><td>Integration</td><td>Test IDoc or message</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SSO and SAP GUI / Fiori entry tested</td><td>Security</td><td>Pilot user logon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Backup job successful on target</td><td>Basis</td><td>Job log</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Monitoring and alerts routed</td><td>Operations</td><td>Test alert received</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hypercare roster published</td><td>Program</td><td>Named shifts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rollback tested or explicitly accepted</td><td>Program</td><td>Decision record</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"closing-view\">Closing view</h2>\n<p>A cloud move is ready when security, sizing, data protection, downtime, and cutover evidence are all owned. Use the table in section 7 as the gate. If a row has no evidence, the landscape is not ready to move, however complete the architecture diagram looks.</p>", "3": "<p>Most SAP Basis candidates fail interviews for a communication reason that sounds like a technical one. They answer with a transaction code when the interviewer asked for a judgment. “I would check SM37” is a step. It is not an answer until you say what you expect to see, what decision it changes, and how you would brief the business if the job cannot be rerun inside the window.</p>\n<p>Zyntegra mock interviews are built to close that gap. The studio on this site uses the same shape: a scenario, a spoken or written response, and a scorecard that names the topics you skipped.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-hiring-managers-are-actually-scoring\">What hiring managers are actually scoring</h2>\n<p>Across consulting firms and enterprise IT teams, the scorecard is remarkably stable.</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Dimension</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>What a strong answer shows</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Structure</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Situation, what you checked, what you changed, how you confirmed</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Technical accuracy</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Correct objects, realistic sequence, no invented transactions</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Risk judgment</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>You protect production before you satisfy curiosity</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Communication</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>A manager can repeat your summary without translating it</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Ownership</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>You say what you would escalate and when</p>\n<p>A candidate who knows every menu path and cannot sequence an incident will lose to a candidate who knows fewer transactions and runs a calm investigation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-part-drill-we-use\">The four-part drill we use</h2>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>1. Context.</strong> The prompt names the system, the time, and the business impact. Example: a critical background job did not run before the warehouse opened. You are the Basis owner. Production is otherwise up.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>2. Response.</strong> You explain your path. We expect a sequence, not a list. Start with impact and scope (one job, one chain, one system), then evidence (SM37 job log, spool, system log, locks, database), then the safe action, then how you inform the business.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>3. Follow-up.</strong> The interviewer changes one fact. The job log shows a missing variant. Or the update processes are stuck. Or the same failure happened after last week’s kernel patch. You must adjust. Repeating the first answer is the most common failure in this phase.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>4. Scorecard.</strong> You receive the topics you covered, the topics you missed, and a model structure you can reuse. The model is not a script to memorize. It is a skeleton: impact, evidence, action, confirmation, communication.</p>\n<h2 id=\"scenarios-that-separate-levels\">Scenarios that separate levels</h2>\n<p>Use these as practice. Answer out loud. Record yourself once. The recording is usually more informative than another hour of reading.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Foundation.</strong> A new dialog user cannot post in a client where colleagues can. Walk through user validity, lock, role assignment, profile comparison, and SU53. Say what you will not do: you will not grant SAP_ALL to “test.”</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Operations.</strong> A transport imported cleanly in quality and failed in production with a return code that points at an object lock or a missing dependency. Explain the checks before a re-import, and why you involve the developer instead of forcing the import during close.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Resilience.</strong> Dialog response time doubled after a refresh. Where do you look first: work processes, expensive statements, missing statistics, wrong buffer parameters copied from production onto a smaller host, or an interface storm from destinations that still point at production?</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Cloud and change.</strong> You are patching kernel in a maintenance window on a cloud-hosted production system. What is snapshotted, who is allowed to decide go or no-go, and what evidence ends the window?</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-speak-so-the-answer-is-reusable\">How to speak so the answer is reusable</h2>\n<p>Adopt a fixed spoken structure. It keeps you from wandering when you are nervous.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Restate the impact</strong> in one sentence so the interviewer knows you heard the business problem.</li>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>State your first check and why.</strong> “I start in SM37 because I need the job status and the step that failed before I touch the variant or the program.”</li>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Name the branch.</strong> “If the log shows an authorization failure, I stop and trace the step user. If it shows a predecessor job still active, I do not restart anything.”</li>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Close with communication.</strong> “I tell the warehouse lead the job will not finish by open, and I give the next update time.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Write this structure at the top of your notes page. Interviewers expect notes. They do not expect you to read a paragraph.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-we-mark-down\">What we mark down</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Listing five transactions with no order.</li>\n<li>Changing production to test a theory, including opening the client or running an unplanned report.</li>\n<li>Blaming “the functional team” with no evidence you looked at the technical layer you own.</li>\n<li>Claiming a certification replaces a project story.</li>\n<li>Inventing a metric. “We improved performance by 40%” is only useful if you can say measured from where, over what period.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-prepare-in-the-ten-days-before-a-real-interview\">How to prepare in the ten days before a real interview</h2>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Day</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Work</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>1–2</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Write five project stories with your actual role, action, and result</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>3–4</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Answer eight scenarios out loud using the four-step structure</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>5</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>One full mock with a timer. Review only the missed topics</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>6–7</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Rebuild weak topics: TMS, refresh, HANA backup, or HA — whichever the scorecard named</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>8</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Second mock. Compare the two scorecards</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>9</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Edit the resume so each bullet could survive the question “what did you personally do?”</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>10</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Rest. Reread only your five stories and your personal checklist</p>\n<h2 id=\"for-corporate-teams\">For corporate teams</h2>\n<p>The same framework trains a bench, not only a job seeker. Run a monthly scenario for the Basis group. Score structure and risk judgment, not speed. Publish the model answer internally after the session so the team converges on one investigation style. Teams that practice incidents before the incident write better tickets during the incident.</p>\n\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>What a strong answer shows</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Structure</td><td>Situation, what you checked, what you changed, how you confirmed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Technical accuracy</td><td>Correct objects, realistic sequence, no invented transactions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Risk judgment</td><td>You protect production before you satisfy curiosity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Communication</td><td>A manager can repeat your summary without translating it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ownership</td><td>You say what you would escalate and when</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"closing-view\">Closing view</h2>\n<p>A mock interview converts when it changes the next answer, not when it flatters the last one. Take the scenario seriously, sequence the evidence, and leave the interviewer with a summary they could paste into a handover. That is the skill production teams hire for.</p>", "4": "<p>Everything senior in SAP Basis sits on five ideas: how the system is built, what a client is, who is allowed to do what, how a change travels, and how you notice that something is wrong before the business does. This article treats those five as one operating picture.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sap-architecture-and-the-system-landscape\">SAP architecture and the system landscape</h2>\n<p>An SAP system is an application server layer plus a database, identified by a system ID (SID). Dialog, background, update, enqueue, and spool work processes share that database. Users do not “connect to a table.” They occupy a work process that executes ABAP (or a related runtime) against the database on their behalf.</p>\n<p>A professional landscape separates risk:</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>System</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Purpose</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>What must never be casual</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Development (DEV)</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Build and unit-level configuration</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Uncontrolled production data</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Quality (QAS)</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Integration test and import rehearsal</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Skipping it to “save a day”</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Production (PRD)</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Business execution</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Experiments, open clients, emergency imports without a record</p>\n<p>Sandbox and training systems are useful. They are not a substitute for a quality system that resembles production in product version, add-ons, and critical interfaces.</p>\n<p>When you explain architecture in an interview or a design review, include: SID, database platform, number of application servers, where the enqueue and message server run, and which system is the transport domain controller. That is a landscape. A list of hostnames is an inventory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sap-gui-clients-and-navigation\">SAP GUI, clients, and navigation</h2>\n<p>The client is a logical partition inside one SAP system. Users, customizing, and much application data are client-dependent. Repository objects (programs, tables definitions, many workbench objects) are client-independent: import them once and every client in that system sees them.</p>\n<p>Consequences you should be able to say out loud:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A user in client 100 is not a user in client 200 until you create or copy that user.</li>\n<li>Customizing recorded in one client does not appear in another until you transport or copy it.</li>\n<li>A workbench transport changes the whole system, not “just the client someone was testing in.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Day-one navigation is not trivia. You should move without searching the menu for: your own user defaults (SU3), locked users and password resets (SU01), authorization trace (SU53 / ST01 with care), system log (SM21), dumps (ST22), work processes (SM50 / SM66), and lock entries (SM12). Speed here is professionalism. It tells a reviewer you have sat in the system.</p>\n<p>Logon groups and message-server load balancing matter as soon as you have more than one application server. Users should not be pointed at a single dialog host by habit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"user-administration-that-survives-an-audit\">User administration that survives an audit</h2>\n<p>User administration is access control, not data entry.</p>\n<p>Create users with a defined validity, a user type that matches the purpose (dialog, system, service, communication, reference), and no shared dialog accounts. System and communication users belong to interfaces and jobs. They should not be used by a person to look around production.</p>\n<p>Roles should be job-based. SAP_ALL and SAP_NEW are emergency or installation tools, not a convenience for a project member who is in a hurry. If you must use a broad profile in a non-production emergency, time-limit it and record the ticket.</p>\n<p>A sound access review checks:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dialog users who have not logged on in the agreed period.</li>\n<li>Users with wide profiles in production.</li>\n<li>Service users with a dialog-capable password policy violation.</li>\n<li>Firefighter or emergency access that was not closed after the incident.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a user reports “I have no authorization,” start with SU53 for the failed check, confirm the client and the user type, and compare with a colleague who can perform the same step. Do not add a profile because the business is waiting. The business is also waiting on the audit six months later.</p>\n<h2 id=\"transport-management-system\">Transport Management System</h2>\n<p>TMS is the change path. Treat it as a safety system.</p>\n<p>The transport domain has a domain controller, typically the production or a dedicated system, that owns the topology. Transport routes describe how a released transport moves, usually DEV to QAS to PRD. Transport groups share a transport directory. If two systems cannot see the same files, the route diagram is fiction.</p>\n<p>A clean change looks like this:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The object is recorded in a transport request in the development client that is open for that change.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>The request is released only when the owner has checked the object list. Releasing “everything in my name” is how unrelated changes reach production together.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>The import into quality is the rehearsal: return code, sequence, and downtime-sensitive objects.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>The production import happens in a window, by a named person, with the queue inspected first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Return codes are part of the language. 0 and 4 need interpretation (4 is a warning you must actually read). 8 and above are failures. Importing again without reading the log is how a landscape drifts.</p>\n<p>Before any production import, know whether the client is closed, whether month-end or a pricing run is in progress, and whether the transport contains table contents that overwrite production customizing. Workbench and customizing requests are not interchangeable risks.</p>\n<p>Client settings (SCC4) deserve the same seriousness. A production client opened for customizing “just for an hour” without a transport is an unsupported change. If the business truly cannot wait for the path, use the emergency process your company defined, then create the transport that documents what happened.</p>\n<h2 id=\"system-monitoring-fundamentals\">System monitoring fundamentals</h2>\n<p>Monitoring is a routine, not a tool purchase. A morning check that takes twenty minutes prevents a noon incident that takes four hours.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Work processes.</strong> Dialog processes all in use means users wait even though the host looks idle in a simple CPU chart. Background processes exhausted means the job chain slips. Update processes stuck means documents hang and the business starts calling about “the system spinning.”</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Dumps (ST22).</strong> Sort by frequency, not by the first dramatic short text. A new dump after a transport is a transport problem until proven otherwise.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>System log (SM21).</strong> Database errors, network reconnects, and gateway failures show here before they become a user complaint.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Update failures (SM13).</strong> An update failure is a business document that did not finish. It is not a Basis curiosity. Identify whether it is one user, one function module, or a full update stop.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Locks (SM12).</strong> Old lock entries, especially after a failed session or a crashed app server, block posting. Remove them only when you know the owner session is gone.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Database and filesystem.</strong> Tablespace or HANA disk, /usr/sap, and the transport directory filling up are classic “the system is down” causes that are completely visible the day before.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Jobs (SM37).</strong> Confirm the house-keeping jobs that your standard build expects: spool cleanup, background job cleanup, and any customer chains that must finish before business open.</p>\n<p>Write the routine down. A checklist that lives in someone’s head leaves with them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-the-five-topics-connect\">How the five topics connect</h2>\n<p>A user cannot post (user administration) in a client that was refreshed badly (clients) because a transport of roles never reached production (TMS), and the dumps have been growing since last night (monitoring), on a system whose update work processes sit on an application server you cannot describe (architecture). Strong consultants do not debug these as five unrelated subjects. They walk the chain.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-practice-this-week\">What to practice this week</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Draw one landscape and mark the domain controller and the transport route.</li>\n<li>In a training system, create a dialog user with a narrow role, reproduce an authorization failure, and resolve it from SU53 without using a wide profile.</li>\n<li>Release a trivial customizing request and import it to the next system. Read the log even if the return code is 0.</li>\n<li>Run the morning checklist for five consecutive days and note what you skipped when you were short on time. The items you skip are the ones to automate or to put first.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"closing-view\">Closing view</h2>\n<p>Architecture, clients, users, transports, and monitoring are the whole job in miniature. If you can explain them with a specific system in mind, later topics — refresh, performance, HANA, cloud — have somewhere to attach. If you cannot, those later topics become vocabulary.</p>", "5": "<p>Operations work is where Basis earns trust. None of these tasks is impressive on a slide. All of them can stop a company when they are done from memory at midnight. The standard below is the one we teach: prepare, change, verify, and leave a record.</p>\n<h2 id=\"installation-and-post-installation\">Installation and post-installation</h2>\n<p>An installation is finished when a named administrator can log on and the system is safe for the next person, not when the installer wizard reports success.</p>\n<p>Before you start, confirm the product availability matrix: operating system, database, kernel, and SAP product versions that are supported together. Confirm hostname, SID, instance numbers, and that they do not collide with an existing system in the landscape. Confirm time synchronization. SAP systems that disagree about time produce certificates, logs, and job schedules you cannot reason about.</p>\n<p>During installation, keep the log. If it fails, the log is the product, not a nuisance.</p>\n<p>Post-installation is the real work:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Apply the license and confirm the installation number and hardware key.</li>\n<li>Set system parameters you refuse to inherit blindly from a template: memory management, login parameters, and password rules appropriate to the tier.</li>\n<li>Create the client strategy. Decide which client is the golden customizing client and which clients are for test users. Do not leave the delivery clients open.</li>\n<li>Configure TMS participation, printers only if needed, and backup of profiles and kernel immediately — before project users arrive.</li>\n<li>Lock or delete sample and SAP\\* behavior according to your security standard. The early administration user is not a permanent shared login.</li>\n<li>Import the support package stack you actually intend to run. “We will patch later” often means the project develops on a stack you will never go live with.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Document the instance profile path, database backup method, and the person who owns the system. An undocumented new system is already drifting.</p>\n<h2 id=\"kernel-and-support-package-upgrades\">Kernel and support package upgrades</h2>\n<p>Upgrades are controlled risk. The note, the stack, and the back-out plan matter more than the speed of SUM or SPAM.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Read before you schedule.</strong> The support package or kernel note states prerequisites, known regressions, and whether a downtime is required. Adjacent components (HANA revision, operating system libraries, SAP Host Agent) often move together. Upgrading the kernel and hoping the database revision is “close enough” is a common outage pattern.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Prepare.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Full backup that you could restore, plus a filesystem copy of kernel and profiles.</li>\n<li>Enough disk for the shadow instance or the upgrade directory, checked with a number, not a glance.</li>\n<li>A locked transport window so nobody releases a change into a system that is halfway through a stack.</li>\n<li>Agreement on the last return point: the clock time at which you restore instead of debugging forward.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Execute in quality first.</strong> Production is the second run. Capture the exact target stack, the duration, and every warning you chose to ignore, with a reason.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Verify.</strong> Work processes start, all instances join, batch jobs resume, RFC destinations answer, and a business pilot can post a document. Technical “green” with a broken interface is not a completed upgrade.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Kernel swaps</strong> specifically: keep the previous kernel directory. A kernel regression is one of the few production problems you can reverse in minutes if you prepared the old kernel and you do not panic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"client-copy-and-system-refresh\">Client copy and system refresh</h2>\n<p>These are different operations. People use the words loosely and then refresh a system they meant only to copy a client inside.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Client copy</strong> (local or remote) duplicates client-dependent data into another client. It is appropriate for creating a test client or aligning customizing. It does not rebuild the database, the kernel, or cross-client repository objects. Repository objects are already shared across clients in that system.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>System refresh</strong> replaces a target system’s data with a copy of another system, usually production into quality. The database moves. The technical identity of the target (SID, hostname, TMS membership) must be put back deliberately. This is why refreshes fail in the week after, not during the restore.</p>\n<p>A professional refresh runbook includes:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Exports you must preserve from the target: RFC destinations, user lists if required, printer definitions, batch jobs, TMS configuration, certificates, and any interface passwords stored in the target.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Source backup or copy method and the time stamp of the data.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Restore into the target.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Post-processing: BDLS or equivalent logical system conversion, RFC rewiring, lock of production users in the non-production copy, scramble or mask sensitive data if policy requires it, reset of batch jobs so production chains do not run against test partners, and correction of printer and file paths.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Validation with the functional team: one document posts, one interface is examined, login works, and the system is not still pointing at production partners.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The most expensive refresh mistake is a quality system that still sends IDocs, payments, or emails to production partners. Suspend outbound interfaces before users are unlocked. Verify, then open.</p>\n<p>Also decide data volume. A full copy of production into a quality host that is half the size will pass the restore and fail the first dialog test. Size the target or reduce the scope honestly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"background-jobs-and-spool\">Background jobs and spool</h2>\n<p>Background processing is application operations that Basis is accountable for keeping healthy.</p>\n<p>Standard house-keeping jobs exist for a reason: deleting old job logs, cleaning spool, and related cleanup. If they are not scheduled, you will eventually fill a filesystem or make SM37 unusable, and you will discover it during an incident you needed SM37 to diagnose.</p>\n<p>When a job fails, read the job log and the spool of the failed step before you restart it. Restarting a job that posted half a financial document, or that failed because a predecessor is still running, doubles the damage. Check:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The step user and its authorizations.</li>\n<li>Variants and whether the variant still exists after a refresh.</li>\n<li>External commands and whether the operating-system path exists on this host.</li>\n<li>Work process type BTC availability (SM50).</li>\n<li>Locks and update failures if the job updates documents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Spool administration is not clerical. A full global spool directory stops printing and can stop work processes that are waiting on spool. Output devices should be named, owned, and tested after every refresh. Access methods and host printers that pointed at production must be reviewed.</p>\n<p>Job scheduling discipline: production jobs have an owner, a calendar, and a monitoring expectation. “It has always run at 01:15” is not a design. Document dependencies. A chain that nobody can draw cannot be recovered calmly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"backup-and-restore-strategies\">Backup and restore strategies</h2>\n<p>A backup you have not restored is a hope. Design the backup around the restore you intend to perform.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>What must be protected</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The database, with a method that meets the RPO (full plus logs, or the HANA equivalent of full, differential, and log backups).</li>\n<li>The SAP kernel and instance profiles.</li>\n<li>The transport directory, because released transports are business changes that may not yet be in every system.</li>\n<li>Certificates and any documented secrets required to rebuild connectivity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Operating principles</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Backups run on a schedule that does not collide with the heaviest batch, or the collision is accepted in writing.</li>\n<li>Failures alert a person. A red icon on a console that nobody watches is not an alert.</li>\n<li>Retention matches legal and operational need. Longer retention is not free; it must still be restorable.</li>\n<li>Non-production systems are backed up to the level their rebuild cost justifies. Development with three weeks of unreleased work and no backup is a project risk, not a technical footnote.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Restore practice</strong></p>\n<p>Once a quarter, or at least twice a year, restore to a scratch system or isolated host. Measure the time to a started SAP instance and to a business-level check. That measured number is your real RTO. The number in the policy document is a wish until then.</p>\n<p>Know the difference between restoring the database onto the same host and rebuilding a host from nothing. Cloud snapshots accelerate the second case only if application-consistent behavior and database log position are understood. A snapshot taken while the database was writing, with no database-aware consistency, can start and then corrupt.</p>\n<p>After any restore into a different SID or hostname, assume you must repeat the refresh post-processing list. The database does not know it has changed identity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"a-single-standard-for-all-five\">A single standard for all five</h2>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Task</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Prepare</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Change</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Verify</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Record</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Install</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Version matrix, SID, time sync</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Software provisioning</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Logon, license, TMS join</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Build sheet</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Upgrade</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Note, backup, freeze</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Quality first, then production</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Pilot business document</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Stack and duration</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Refresh</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Export target config, suspend interfaces</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Restore</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>BDLS, RFC, users, one posting</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Data timestamp</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Jobs</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Dependency list</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Schedule or restart with reason</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Job log and business result</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Owner and calendar</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Backup</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>RPO and scope</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Scheduled, monitored job</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Periodic restore test</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Last successful restore date</p>\n\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table>\n<thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Prepare</th><th>Change</th><th>Verify</th><th>Record</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Install</td><td>Version matrix, SID, time sync</td><td>Software provisioning</td><td>Logon, license, TMS join</td><td>Build sheet</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Upgrade</td><td>Note, backup, freeze</td><td>Quality first, then production</td><td>Pilot business document</td><td>Stack and duration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Refresh</td><td>Export target config, suspend interfaces</td><td>Restore</td><td>BDLS, RFC, users, one posting</td><td>Data timestamp</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jobs</td><td>Dependency list</td><td>Schedule or restart with reason</td><td>Job log and business result</td><td>Owner and calendar</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Backup</td><td>RPO and scope</td><td>Scheduled, monitored job</td><td>Periodic restore test</td><td>Last successful restore date</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"closing-view\">Closing view</h2>\n<p>Installation, patching, refresh, batch, and backup are one discipline: change the system only when you can return, and prove the result with a business check. Teams that write these five runbooks spend their incidents on genuine surprises. Teams that do not spend their incidents rediscovering the runbook.</p>", "6": "<p>A landscape that merely starts is not an enterprise landscape. The next standard is whether it stays fast, survives a failed host, can be diagnosed from evidence, and would pass an access review. This article is the operating view Zyntegra expects at advanced level.</p>\n<h2 id=\"performance-tuning-and-workload-analysis\">Performance tuning and workload analysis</h2>\n<p>Start with a definition. “The system is slow” is not a symptom you can tune. Ask who is affected, since when, in which transaction, and whether background jobs are late as well. A single report and a system-wide stall are different problems.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Work process view.</strong> If every dialog process is busy, new users wait. Identify whether those processes are stuck on the database, on RFC, on a lock, or on CPU. Killing sessions is a last resort after you know what business document you are interrupting.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Database view.</strong> Expensive statements, missing indexes or statistics after a refresh, and unbounded selects are the usual database-side causes. Capture the statement, the time, and the program before you propose an index. An index that speeds one report and slows every goods movement is not a tuning win.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Memory and buffers.</strong> SAP memory parameters must match the host. A profile copied from a large production host onto a smaller quality host will page or fail in ways that look like application bugs. After any move or resize, review instance parameters against actual RAM.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Change correlation.</strong> The fastest diagnostic question is “what changed?” A transport, a kernel, a statistics job that did not run, a new interface, or a month-end variant. Performance work without a timeline becomes superstition.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>What good looks like.</strong> You keep a small baseline: average dialog response in a normal hour, a handful of critical jobs and their usual duration, and database CPU in that same hour. You compare the incident to the baseline. You change one thing at a time in production.</p>\n<p>Avoid these habits: adding application servers to hide an expensive SQL statement, running an SQL trace on a busy production system with no filter, and declaring victory because CPU dropped while users are still waiting on locks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"high-availability-and-disaster-recovery\">High availability and disaster recovery</h2>\n<p>High availability (HA) keeps the service running through a component failure. Disaster recovery (DR) restores the service in another site or region after the first site is lost. They are designed separately and tested separately.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Know the single points of failure</strong> in your actual build: database host, enqueue server, message server, central file mounts for /sapmnt and the transport directory, web dispatcher, and identity. A second application server does not protect you from a database disk failure.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>SAP application layer.</strong> Additional dialog instances, a redundant web dispatcher, and enqueue replication (or the current recommended mechanism for your product version) are the usual patterns. Document which instance users enter through. Bypassing the load balancer “temporarily” becomes the undocumented production path.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Database layer.</strong> For SAP HANA this commonly means system replication to a secondary, with a tested takeover. Replication that has never failed over is an unproven design. Measure time to takeover and time until a user can post. Include the application servers’ ability to find the database after the virtual hostname moves.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>DR.</strong> A replica in another region still needs network, DNS or hostname strategy, interface endpoints, and backup. RPO is how much data you can lose (replication mode and any async lag). RTO is how long until service. Write both numbers next to the last test date.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Operational rules.</strong> Patch HA components deliberately. Monitor replication lag as a first-class alert. Do not snapshot or freeze a primary in a way that stalls replication through a business peak without a decision. After takeover, know whether you are running unprotected, and what it takes to rebuild the pair.</p>\n<p>A tabletop exercise every six months is the minimum: “the database host is gone, it is Tuesday 10:00, payroll runs Thursday.” If the team cannot name the first five actions, the architecture diagram is ahead of the team.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sap-hana-administration-for-basis\">SAP HANA administration for Basis</h2>\n<p>You do not need to be a full-time HANA engineer to be a competent Basis consultant on S/4HANA. You do need to operate the database safely.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Services and tenants.</strong> Know which tenant is the application database, how you start and stop the system in the supported order, and what HANA Studio, cockpit, or SQL console your company actually uses. Stopping the wrong tenant is a résumé-level incident.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Memory.</strong> HANA is an in-memory platform. A host that meets the vCPU count and misses the memory or the storage throughput will not “cache its way out.” Understand the difference between data volume, log volume, and backup volume. Filling the log volume stops the database.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Backup.</strong> Full, differential or incremental as designed, plus log backups that meet the RPO. Confirm the backup catalog is itself recoverable. Practice recovering to a point in time on a non-production system.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Alerts.</strong> Act on disk, backup age, crash dumps, and replication state. A cockpit full of unowned warnings trains everyone to ignore the one alert that matters.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Housekeeping.</strong> Unload and merge behavior, trace files, and old diagnosis files will fill disks. Schedule cleanup that is approved for your revision; do not delete files because a forum post said they looked unused.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Mini checks and growth.</strong> Know how to see largest tables and whether growth is business volume or a custom program writing without a retention rule. That conversation belongs with the application owner, with numbers attached.</p>\n<p>When you talk to a cloud team, translate HANA needs into certified shapes, storage layout, and backup service. “Just give it a big VM” is not a HANA design.</p>\n<h2 id=\"solution-manager-and-monitoring-that-someone-owns\">Solution Manager and monitoring that someone owns</h2>\n<p>Tooling changes — Solution Manager, Focused Run, cloud observability, Grafana, Dynatrace — but the requirement does not. Every alert needs an owner and a response.</p>\n<p>A useful SAP technical monitor covers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Instance availability and work-process health.</li>\n<li>Update failures and dump rate, with a threshold based on your baseline, not a vendor default you have never read.</li>\n<li>Database backup age and disk.</li>\n<li>Interface channel failures for the flows the business named as critical.</li>\n<li>Certificate expiry with thirty days of warning, not on the morning of expiry.</li>\n<li>Replication or cluster state for HA.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>EarlyWatch-style reviews still earn their place: they catch parameter drift, missing housekeeping, and performance trends that a red/green dashboard hides. Read the report. Assign three actions. Close them. A library of unread PDFs is not monitoring.</p>\n<p>Suppressing an alert permanently because it is noisy is acceptable only when you record why it is safe. Otherwise you have trained the console to lie.</p>\n<p>Integrate the morning checklist from daily operations with the tool. The tool should remove the need to click SM51 by hand. It should not remove the need for a person who understands what the click used to show.</p>\n<h2 id=\"security-hardening-and-patching\">Security hardening and patching</h2>\n<p>Hardening is a set of decisions you can show an auditor and still run the business.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Access.</strong> Remove SAP_ALL from daily use. Implement emergency access with a log. Review powerful roles on a calendar. Lock the production client against ad-hoc customizing.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Password and logon policy.</strong> Failed logon limits, password length, and idle timeout appropriate to the tier. Do not apply a laboratory-strict policy that creates shared passwords on stickers; fix the process instead.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>RFC and gateway.</strong> Restrict external RFC registration. Do not leave a gateway open to the network because an old interface might need it. Inventory destinations after every refresh.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Web and certificates.</strong> Web dispatcher or reverse proxy in front of HTTP services, TLS current, STRUST certificates owned and renewed. Internal HTTP to the application server from anywhere on the corporate network is a habit, not a design.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Patch cadence.</strong> Security notes are triaged: applicable or not, workaround or not, emergency or monthly window. Kernel and Host Agent stay on a supported level. “We only patch twice a year” is a risk acceptance that should be signed by someone who can accept it, not implied by a busy backlog.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Audit evidence.</strong> User reviews, emergency access logs, client-opening tickets, and transport approvals. If it is not recorded, it did not happen, even if you remember doing it.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Least drama.</strong> Hardening that breaks the payroll interface on a Friday is not a success. Change security with the same prepare–verify loop as a kernel upgrade.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-these-topics-show-up-in-one-incident\">How these topics show up in one incident</h2>\n<p>A slow production system after a host failover might be: replication took over (HA), the application servers still point at the old database listener (HANA and HA together), expensive SQL is now visible because statistics moved (performance), the monitoring alert was suppressed last quarter (monitoring), and the only person who can see the database uses a shared admin user (security). One incident, five topics. That is why they are taught together.</p>\n<h2 id=\"closing-view\">Closing view</h2>\n<p>Performance, resilience, HANA operations, owned monitoring, and hardening are the difference between a system that exists and a system you can put a company on. Measure a baseline, test failover, back up HANA to a proven restore, give every alert an owner, and patch on purpose. The advanced consultant is the person who can do those things without turning them into a project each time.</p>", "7": "<p>Expert Basis work is still administration, practiced inside programs that have many workstreams. You will be asked to place SAP on a public cloud, to prepare a system for S/4HANA, to stop doing refresh post-processing by hand, to support BTP connectivity, and to lead the technical side of an outage. This article is how those five responsibilities fit one role.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sap-on-azure-aws-and-google-cloud\">SAP on Azure, AWS, and Google Cloud</h2>\n<p>The application does not become simpler because the server is virtual. You still own start order, profiles, transports, backup, and performance. You share the platform with a cloud team. Write the boundary down or you will argue about it during the incident.</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Layer</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Typical cloud owner</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Typical Basis owner</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Subscription, account, network, identity for administrators</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Cloud / platform</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Requirements and sign-off</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Virtual machines, disks, load balancers</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Cloud, from a Basis design</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>SAP sizing and storage layout</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Operating system hardening and patching</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Shared; agree the window</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Impact on SAP and HANA</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Database backup service vs. HANA backup</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Platform capability</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Backup design, catalog, restore test</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>SAP kernel, clients, TMS, jobs, interfaces</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Basis</p>\n<p>|   |</p>\n<p>| - |</p>\n<p>Basis</p>\n<p>Design rules that survive all three providers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use certified shapes for HANA and for the SAP product version you run. Confirm the current note; do not reuse a diagram from a previous program.</li>\n<li>Put data, log, and shared SAP files on storage that meets the throughput the sizing exercise produced. A cheap disk that passes a boot test fails a month-end posting test.</li>\n<li>Private connectivity for administrators and for interface partners. Public IP addresses on a database are a defect.</li>\n<li>Time sync, DNS, and a hostname strategy that HA can move.</li>\n<li>Backup and monitoring hooked to the same on-call rotation as today. A cloud console login that only one architect possesses is not an operating model.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Learn one provider deeply. Be able to read the others. Interviewers would rather hear a precise Azure landing-zone story than three vague comparisons.</p>\n<h2 id=\"s-4hana-conversion-readiness\">S/4HANA conversion readiness</h2>\n<p>A conversion is not a Basis weekend with a larger kernel. It is a program. Basis still owns a workstream that can delay everyone else.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Technical inventory.</strong> Source release, database (any conversion target of consequence is SAP HANA), add-ons and whether each vendor supports the target, dual-stack leftovers, and custom code volume. Unsupported add-ons are schedule risk, not a footnote.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Simplification and custom code.</strong> The simplification item list and the custom-code analysis tell you what will not behave as before. Basis does not rewrite the code. Basis does make sure the analysis system exists, the extract ran, and the findings are assigned to owners.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Landscape path.</strong> You need a sandbox that can be converted more than once. The first sandbox is where the project learns. Production is where it executes. Plan refreshes of that sandbox from production so the second rehearsal is not a fantasy based on last year’s data.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Downtime.</strong> Software Update Manager downtime depends on data volume, archiving discipline, and the approach (standard conversion, downtime-optimized options where they apply). Quote a number only after a rehearsal on comparable hardware. Add time for interfaces, not only for SUM.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Operational readiness.</strong> Roles change, Fiori entry points appear, and background jobs from the ECC era may be obsolete or harmful. Include job review, printer and output, RFC, and SSO in the cutover checklist. The technical convert can succeed and the business can still be unable to log on through the new URL.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Skills.</strong> The Basis consultant on a conversion can explain SUM at a high level, HANA prerequisites, add-on constraints, and the rehearsal evidence. Pretending to be the functional lead and the developer as well helps nobody.</p>\n<h2 id=\"landscape-automation\">Landscape automation</h2>\n<p>Automation is how you make the runbooks in the operations articles repeatable. It is not a separate hobby.</p>\n<p>Good candidates:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Post-refresh steps that are identical every time: suspend interfaces, run logical-system conversion, lock source users, reset specific jobs, and mail the functional lead a checklist.</li>\n<li>Health checks you already do each morning, posted to a channel the team reads.</li>\n<li>Kernel and host-agent rollout on non-production, with production still in a controlled window.</li>\n<li>Certificate expiry collection.</li>\n<li>Building a sandbox from a standard image plus a current backup, instead of a wiki page titled “ask Ravi.”</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Good practice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The automated step has a manual fallback you have used.</li>\n<li>Secrets live in a store, not in the script repository.</li>\n<li>The script logs what it changed.</li>\n<li>Non-production is the first consumer. Production automation is adopted after the script has failed in quality and been fixed.</li>\n<li>You can explain the script without reading it line by line. If only the author can run it, you have not automated the landscape; you have concentrated the risk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Infrastructure as code for the cloud layer (networks, hosts, disks) belongs with the platform team and should be readable by Basis. Basis-as-code for SAP-specific post-processing can live with the Basis team. Agree the split so both sides are not editing the same playbook in ignorance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"integration-with-sap-btp-and-cloud-integration\">Integration with SAP BTP and Cloud Integration</h2>\n<p>Basis is often the team who gets the call when Cloud Integration cannot reach the on-premise system. You should be able to support that call without claiming to be the integration developer.</p>\n<p>Know the pieces:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Subaccount and destination.</strong> A destination is a named connection (URL, authentication, proxy). Wrong URL after a cloud migration is the most common failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Connector.</strong> It runs in your network and opens a tunnel outbound to BTP. If it is down, unregistered, or pointing at the old location ID, every dependent interface fails while the S/4 system looks healthy.</li>\n<li><strong>Certificates and principal propagation.</strong> STRUST on the SAP side, trust on the BTP side, and clocks in sync. Expired certificates fail more often than mapping logic.</li>\n<li><strong>Cloud Integration (CPI) message monitor.</strong> You can see whether a message failed on the cloud side or never arrived. That single distinction saves hours of argument.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your runbook should include: who owns the connector host, how you restart it, where the certificate expiry is monitored, and how a refresh of the S/4 system is reflected in destinations. Refreshes that restore yesterday’s RFC and destination data will quietly point a non-production subaccount at production, or the reverse. Treat BTP destinations as refresh post-processing.</p>\n<p>You are not expected to design every iFlow. You are expected to keep the path up and to know which team owns the mapping when the path is up and the payload is wrong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"production-war-room-scenarios\">Production war-room scenarios</h2>\n<p>A war room is a temporary operating system for a serious incident. The goal is restoration of service and a clear record, not a performance of urgency.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Open the room with four facts.</strong> What is impacted, since when, what changed in the last 24 hours, and who is the single incident lead. If everyone is investigating, nobody is coordinating.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Roles.</strong> One incident lead (often Basis for a technical outage). One person communicating to the business on a clock, for example every 30 minutes. One log keeper. Specialists join and leave. Executives receive the communication; they do not drive the keyboard.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Sequence.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stabilize. Stop further damage: lock users if corruption is suspected, stop a job that is making it worse, fail over if that is the tested path.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Gather evidence before you change more. Work processes, system log, dumps, database alert, recent transports, recent infrastructure events.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Form one hypothesis and test it. Parallel theories without an owner create conflicting changes.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Recover and verify with a business transaction, not only with a green process list.</li>\n<ol>\n<li>Keep hypercare on that symptom for an agreed period.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Conduct.</strong> No undocumented production changes. No blame in the bridge call. Side investigations happen in a side channel and report back to the lead. Time stamps are written in one time zone.</p>\n<p class=\"strongline\"><strong>Afterward.</strong> Within two business days, a short review: timeline, cause, what detected it, what would have detected it sooner, and which runbook changed. A war room that does not change a runbook will be repeated with the same script.</p>\n<p>Practice one scenario a quarter. The useful scenarios are specific: update mechanism stopped, HANA log volume full, failed production import during close, Cloud Connector down before a payroll interface, failed failover where the application server still points at the old host. Generic “the system is down” practice teaches panic. Specific practice teaches sequence.</p>"};
const meta={"1": {"title": "A practical SAP Basis career roadmap for 2026", "cat": "ROADMAP"}, "2": {"title": "Enterprise cloud migration checklist for SAP landscapes", "cat": "CLOUD"}, "3": {"title": "How we run SAP Basis mock interviews that convert", "cat": "INTERVIEWS"}, "4": {"title": "SAP architecture, clients, users, transports, and daily monitoring", "cat": "FOUNDATIONS"}, "5": {"title": "Installation, upgrades, system refresh, jobs, and backup", "cat": "OPERATIONS"}, "6": {"title": "Performance, high availability, HANA, monitoring, and security hardening", "cat": "RESILIENCE"}, "7": {"title": "Cloud operations, S/4HANA readiness, automation, BTP, and production war rooms", "cat": "TRANSFORMATION"}};
const reader=document.getElementById("reader"), content=document.getElementById("readerContent"), readerCat=document.getElementById("readerCat");
function openArticle(id){
  const m=meta[id]; if(!m)return;
  readerCat.textContent=m.cat+" • 0"+id;
  content.innerHTML="<h1>"+m.title+"</h1>"+articles[id];
  reader.classList.add("show"); document.body.style.overflow="hidden";
  reader.querySelector(".reader-box").scrollTop=0;
}
function closeArticle(){reader.classList.remove("show");document.body.style.overflow="";}
document.querySelectorAll(".article-card,.visual-card").forEach(el=>el.addEventListener("click",()=>openArticle(el.dataset.article)));
document.getElementById("closeReader").addEventListener("click",closeArticle);
document.querySelector(".reader-backdrop").addEventListener("click",closeArticle);
document.addEventListener("keydown",e=>{if(e.key==="Escape"){closeArticle();closeSearch();}});
document.getElementById("themeBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>document.body.classList.toggle("light"));
const cards=[...document.querySelectorAll(".article-card")];
const filterButtons=[...document.querySelectorAll(".filters button")];
function applyFilter(){
  const q=document.getElementById("search").value.toLowerCase().trim();
  const active=document.querySelector(".filters button.active")?.dataset.filter||"ALL";
  cards.forEach(c=>{
    const matchText=c.dataset.search.includes(q);
    const matchCat=active==="ALL"||c.querySelector(".cat").textContent===active;
    c.style.display=matchText&&matchCat?"block":"none";
  });
}
document.getElementById("search").addEventListener("input",applyFilter);
filterButtons.forEach(b=>b.addEventListener("click",()=>{filterButtons.forEach(x=>x.classList.remove("active"));b.classList.add("active");applyFilter();}));

const panel=document.getElementById("searchPanel");
function closeSearch(){panel.classList.remove("show");}
document.getElementById("searchBtn").addEventListener("click",()=>{panel.classList.add("show");setTimeout(()=>document.getElementById("globalSearch").focus(),50);});
document.getElementById("closeSearch").addEventListener("click",closeSearch);
const global=document.getElementById("globalSearch"), results=document.getElementById("searchResults");
function globalSearch(q){
  q=q.toLowerCase().trim();
  if(!q){results.innerHTML="";return;}
  results.innerHTML=Object.entries(meta).filter(([id,m])=>(m.title+" "+m.cat).toLowerCase().includes(q))
    .map(([id,m])=>`<div class="result" data-id="${id}"><b>${m.title}</b><span>${m.cat} • Open article</span></div>`).join("")||"<p style='color:#8098a8'>No article matched that search.</p>";
  results.querySelectorAll(".result").forEach(x=>x.addEventListener("click",()=>{closeSearch();openArticle(x.dataset.id);}));
}
global.addEventListener("input",e=>globalSearch(e.target.value));
window.addEventListener("scroll",()=>{
  const d=document.documentElement;
  const pct=(d.scrollTop/(d.scrollHeight-d.clientHeight))*100;
  document.getElementById("progress").style.width=pct+"%";
});
